WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.960
Sequels to movies will always&nbsp;
come with its bumps in the road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.160
but for Jake Sully? He may have a bit&nbsp;
of a boring, confusing story problem.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:14.760
After 13 years, "Avatar: The Way of Water" has&nbsp;
brought viewers back to Pandora and into the life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:20.520
of ex-Marine-turned-full-time-blue-person Jake&nbsp;
Sully. Reactions to "Avatar 2" have been strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.500
and for good reason — it's visually stunning,&nbsp;
utterly immersive, and far better on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.580
narrative front than its predecessor. In&nbsp;
short, it's everything a sequel should be.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.960
Given how much cutting-edge technology&nbsp;
James Cameron brought to bear for the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.140
it should be no surprise that&nbsp;
"The Way of Water" looks stunning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.720
and unlike any other film ever made before.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.540
By leaping into the future and giving Jake and&nbsp;
Neytiri a whole family to look out for, "Avatar 2"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:51.060
adds several meaningful layers to the narrative.&nbsp;
The result is a relatively simple but emotionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:56.880
affecting story about parenthood, growing up, and&nbsp;
protecting the things you love. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.500
"The Way of Water" is still built on the&nbsp;
shaky foundation that is the original movie.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.520
“And I was hoping for some tactical&nbsp;
plan that didn’t involve martyrdom.”

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.560
This doesn't hurt the film too badly when it's&nbsp;
focused on new characters or big cinematic set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.140
pieces, but it becomes glaringly obvious whenever&nbsp;
Jake is back in the spotlight. While the reformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.960
jarhead is markedly better in the sequel, he can't&nbsp;
escape the problems of his past. Despite its best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.820
efforts, "Avatar: The Way of Water" can't&nbsp;
shake its predecessor's Jake Sully problem.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:32.460
The sad fact is that Jake Sully is a terrible&nbsp;
protagonist — and barely half a character — in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.680
original "Avatar." Sam Worthington does his best&nbsp;
to bring some life and charm to the ex-marine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.840
but the material he's given to work with is just&nbsp;
pitiful. For those who haven't seen the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.000
since its original release in 2009, let's&nbsp;
do a little recap of Jake's character arc.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.580
Paraplegic ex-marine Jake Sully arrives on&nbsp;
Pandora to take the place of his scientist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.540
twin brother, whose avatar was fully&nbsp;
completed before his untimely death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:01.140
Jake is given the opportunity&nbsp;
to learn the ways of the Na'vi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:05.340
but he's actually spying on them for the&nbsp;
Resources Development Administration.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.080
After spending three months without once&nbsp;
mentioning the impending danger to their land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.820
Jake is cast out by Neytiri when&nbsp;
the humans bulldoze Hometree. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:18.780
then tames a big bird that only five&nbsp;
Na'vi in history have ever ridden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:24.360
immediately regains the full trust of the clan,&nbsp;
and leads them to victory as their new chosen one.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:29.640
Aside from being grossly white savior-y and&nbsp;
pretty ableist, Jake's story just flat-out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:35.340
doesn't make sense. The only reason he's welcomed&nbsp;
as a hero by the Na'vi is that he tames the toruk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.440
but doing so is so simple that it drains the&nbsp;
act of any weight. We learn nothing about his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.760
past besides his military service record,&nbsp;
and we're never given a real reason to root&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.960
for him. We're told that Jake is the main&nbsp;
character, but he doesn't earn the title.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.260
The good news is that Jake is&nbsp;
way more interesting in "Avatar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:56.940
The Way of Water." The bad news is that the&nbsp;
film can't fully fix his problems from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:01.620
first film. Making Jake a protective father&nbsp;
who's scared for his kids is a great move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.120
and it leads to a far more compelling arc&nbsp;
than the one in the first movie. But James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.320
Cameron still wants us to believe that Jake is&nbsp;
the hero of heroes, and it just doesn't work.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.480
The Sullys' arrival on the reefs of the Metkayina&nbsp;
would have been a perfect opportunity to unpack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:21.000
some things from the first film. Clan leaders&nbsp;
Ronal and Tonowari are hesitant at first to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.440
let them stay, pointing out their mixed-blood&nbsp;
features as a reason that they wouldn't fit in.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.540
However, they stop short of questioning&nbsp;
Jake's legitimacy as a Na'vi leader. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.220
would make sense for Ronal to ask some&nbsp;
questions like why are so many Na'vi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:40.080
following this guy? Didn't he just lie to us&nbsp;
for three months and then ride a giant bird?

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.420
But posing those questions would be putting all&nbsp;
of "Avatar" into question, which is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:51.240
Cameron clearly isn't willing to do. Granted, it's&nbsp;
been nearly two decades of Jake proving himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.380
but Tonowari would still be right to&nbsp;
remain suspicious of Jake's origins.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:01.260
Yes, Jake humbles himself a bit and becomes&nbsp;
more vulnerable in "Avatar 2," but it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:05.880
enough to redeem his character. He still&nbsp;
refers to himself as a "dumb grunt," so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:11.340
why is he received like a legendary leader?&nbsp;
Maybe Cameron just can't let go of the heroic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:18.180
space marine myth. But given how outright evil&nbsp;
the RDA is, that message is confused at best.

